// progressRing(attrs: List<Attr<M>>) -> Element<M>: an element of the class ProgressRing. A
// ring that shows that something is in progress (WinUI's `ProgressRing`): a spinning arc while
// [`is_indeterminate`](Self::is_indeterminate) (the default), the part of a ring of the
// [`value`](Self::value) between [`minimum`](Self::minimum) and [`maximum`](Self::maximum)
// otherwise, and nothing while not [`is_active`](Self::is_active). As in WinUI, `Value`,
// `Minimum` and `Maximum` are properties of the ring (not of `RangeBase`), coerced as
// `ProgressRing.cpp` coerces them: the bound set last wins, and a value outside the range is
// moved into it. The control sets the states of WinUI as pseudo-classes, which the control
// theme of the WinUI theme (`src/FerroUI.Themes.WinUI/Controls/ProgressRing.xaml`) styles:
// `:active` (Active), `:determinate-active` (DeterminateActive) and `:inactive` (Inactive).
// WinUI draws the ring with an `AnimatedVisualPlayer` and the Lottie compositions of its
// animated visuals, which the code plays. FerroUI has no player: the template draws the ring
// with `Arc` shapes, and the control moves the arc of the template part `PART_Indicator` as
// the code of WinUI plays the compositions: the indeterminate composition loops over two
// seconds (the arc grows to half the ring and shrinks while it turns 900 degrees), and a
// determinate change plays the determinate composition from the old to the new value (two
// seconds for the whole ring), a decrease at once. The clock is the ring's `Clock`, or the
// global clock; the motion stops while the ring is inactive, hidden or out of the visual tree.
// `DeterminateSource` and `IndeterminateSource` (preview members of WinUI) are not ported. The
// Fluent and Simple themes have no template for it (docs/porting/winui-controls.md, section
// 5.1).
fn progressRing(attrs) = Element { elementType: 306, elementAttrs: attrs, elementChildren: [] }
